
update products set
  quick_answer = 'The Synedica Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit supplies tirzepatide (CAS 2023788-19-2), a 39-amino-acid dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonist peptide studied in Phase 3 clinical trials for metabolic, cardiovascular and liver research. Synedica UK offers this kit for sale strictly for laboratory and in-vitro research use, not as a licensed medicine.',
  content_sections = '[{"heading": null, "body": "Tirzepatide''s headline weight-loss trial results have made it one of the most searched-for research peptides in the UK, and with that demand comes the same problem seen across the category: listings that copy a purity percentage from one supplier to the next without batch-specific evidence behind it. Researchers who source peptides regularly know to check the batch number, the testing method, and to treat a headline trial result as a description of the licensed drug in clinical use — not a guarantee about what''s actually in a given vial. This page sets out what the <strong>Synedica Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit</strong> is, what current clinical research has found, and how to verify what you''re buying before it goes anywhere near the bench."}, {"heading": "What Tirzepatide Is", "body": "Tirzepatide is a single-molecule, 39-amino-acid peptide and dual receptor agonist, designed to activate both the glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP) receptor and the glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor. It carries CAS number 2023788-19-2, molecular formula C225H348N48O68, and a molecular weight of approximately 4,813.5 g/mol. Combining two incretin-receptor targets in one molecule distinguishes it from single-target GLP-1 agonists such as semaglutide, and it is structurally and pharmacologically distinct from newer triple agonists such as retatrutide."}, {"heading": "What Current Research Says", "body": "Tirzepatide''s pivotal Phase 3 obesity trial, SURMOUNT-1, was published in the New England Journal of Medicine on 4 June 2022. Across 2,539 participants over 72 weeks, the highest studied dose (15 mg weekly) produced a mean body-weight reduction of 22.5%, compared with 2.4% on placebo, with lead investigator Ania Jastreboff noting that \"about nine out of ten participants taking tirzepatide lost weight\" (<a href=\"https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/lillys-surmount-1-results-published-new-england-journal-medicine\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jastreboff et al., 2022, N Engl J Med</a>).<br /><br />A later head-to-head Phase 3b trial, SURMOUNT-5, reported on 10 July 2025, compared tirzepatide directly against semaglutide in 751 participants over 72 weeks. Tirzepatide produced a mean weight reduction of 20.2% versus 13.7% for semaglutide, and a waist-circumference reduction of 18.4 cm versus 13.0 cm (<a href=\"https://www.acc.org/Latest-in-Cardiology/Journal-Scans/2025/07/10/09/09/SURMOUNT-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">American College of Cardiology, 10 July 2025</a>).<br /><br />A large real-world cohort study published in Hepatology International on 3 July 2026 matched 43,743 tirzepatide initiators against an equal number of SGLT2-inhibitor users with metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) using the TriNetX network. At one-year follow-up, the tirzepatide group showed lower all-cause mortality (0.48% vs 1.38%, hazard ratio 0.328) and fewer major adverse cardiovascular events (hazard ratio 0.506) than the SGLT2-inhibitor group (<a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12072-026-11122-3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Khalil & Rahman et al., 2026, Hepatology International</a>). None of these findings constitute a treatment claim for this research-use product; they describe what has been studied in registered clinical trials and real-world cohorts of the molecule, not what any laboratory-use compound sold by Synedica UK does for an individual."}, {"heading": "Tirzepatide vs Related Research Peptides", "body": "<strong>Synedica Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit</strong> is one of several incretin-pathway peptides studied in metabolic research; the table below summarises how its chemical identity compares with two related compounds also supplied by Synedica UK for laboratory use.<br /><br /><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding:6px 8px;\">Property</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding:6px 8px;\">Tirzepatide</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding:6px 8px;\">Retatrutide</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding:6px 8px;\">Semaglutide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Receptor targets</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">GIP, GLP-1 (dual)</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">GLP-1, GIP, glucagon (triple)</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">GLP-1 (single)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">CAS number</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">2023788-19-2</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">2381089-83-2</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">910463-68-2</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Molecular formula</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">C225H348N48O68</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">C221H342N46O68</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">C187H291N45O59</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Molecular weight</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">≈4,813.5 g/mol</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">≈4,731.4 g/mol</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">≈4,113.6 g/mol</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;\">Amino acid count</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;\">39</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;\">39</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;\">31</td></tr></tbody></table><br />Batch-specific purity, storage conditions and testing method are confirmed on each product''s Certificate of Analysis rather than assumed from the class average — see the purity section below. The <a href=\"/product/synedica-retatrutide-40mg-injection-kit\">Synedica Retatrutide 40mg Injection Kit</a> and <a href=\"/product/synedica-semaglutide-glp-1-pen-kit\">Synedica Semaglutide GLP-1 Pen Kit</a> pages carry their own batch documentation."}, {"heading": "Purity and Certificate of Analysis Standards", "body": "Every <strong>Synedica Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit</strong> offered for sale is supplied with third-party analytical documentation rather than a single purity percentage repeated across listings. A Certificate of Analysis (COA) should show the specific batch or lot number, the analytical method used (typically HPLC or mass spectrometry), the measured purity result for that batch, and the issuing laboratory. Given how widely tirzepatide purity claims vary across UK vendor listings, researchers should treat a listing without batch-specific COA access as a red flag rather than an oversight."}, {"heading": "Sourcing and Verification", "body": "Buying a high-demand compound like tirzepatide from a UK-based supplier does not remove the need to verify what arrives. Before ordering, check that the listing states research-use status clearly, that batch documentation is offered rather than promised only “on request,” and that the compound identity (CAS number, molecular formula) is independently verifiable against a source such as PubChem. The <strong>Synedica Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit</strong> is offered for sale on this basis, with documentation available per batch rather than as a one-off marketing claim."}]'::jsonb,
  faq = '[{"question": "What is the Synedica Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit used for?", "answer": "It supplies tirzepatide, a dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonist peptide, for laboratory and in-vitro research into incretin-receptor pharmacology and metabolic signalling pathways. It is not sold for any therapeutic, dietary, or performance use."}, {"question": "Is it legal to buy tirzepatide as a research chemical in the UK?", "answer": "UK researchers and laboratories can lawfully purchase compounds such as tirzepatide when they are supplied and marketed strictly for research or in-vitro use rather than as a medicine. This product is not licensed under the <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2012/1916\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Human Medicines Regulations 2012 (SI 2012/1916)</a> and has not been assessed by the MHRA for human safety or efficacy under that route, so it must not be purchased for human or veterinary use."}, {"question": "What does the Certificate of Analysis (COA) show?", "answer": "A COA should confirm the batch number, the analytical testing method (typically HPLC or mass spectrometry), the measured purity for that specific batch, and the issuing laboratory, allowing a researcher to verify what a given batch actually contains rather than relying on a generic listing description."}, {"question": "How is the CAS number used to verify this compound?", "answer": "CAS 2023788-19-2 identifies tirzepatide specifically. Researchers can cross-check this number, the molecular formula (C225H348N48O68), and the molecular weight (approximately 4,813.5 g/mol) against an independent database such as PubChem to confirm chemical identity before use."}, {"question": "How does tirzepatide differ from retatrutide and semaglutide?", "answer": "Tirzepatide activates two receptors — GIP and GLP-1 — while retatrutide activates three (GLP-1, GIP and glucagon) and semaglutide activates one (GLP-1 only). All three are peptides studied in incretin-pathway research, but they are distinct molecules with different CAS numbers, molecular weights, and receptor-binding profiles; see the comparison table above."}, {"question": "What has clinical research found about tirzepatide so far?", "answer": "Registered Phase 3 trials, published in journals including the New England Journal of Medicine, and large real-world cohort studies, have examined tirzepatide''s effects on body weight, cardiovascular events and liver-related outcomes in study populations. These findings describe outcomes recorded in formal clinical trials and observational research on the molecule; they are not claims about this research-use product, which is not licensed as a medicine."}, {"question": "How does tirzepatide compare with semaglutide in head-to-head research?", "answer": "The SURMOUNT-5 Phase 3b trial, reported 10 July 2025, compared the two molecules directly in 751 participants over 72 weeks and found tirzepatide produced a greater mean weight reduction (20.2% vs 13.7%) and greater waist-circumference reduction (18.4 cm vs 13.0 cm) than semaglutide in that trial population."}, {"question": "Does the product form (injection pen kit) affect the research application?", "answer": "The pen kit format is a delivery/packaging format only. This listing makes no dosing, administration, or reconstitution recommendations; researchers determine their own protocols in line with their institution''s research and safety governance."}, {"question": "How should the Synedica Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit be stored before use?", "answer": "Store according to the conditions printed on the batch documentation and product label, generally in cool, dry, dark conditions. Exact storage parameters and shelf life are batch-specific and confirmed on the accompanying documentation."}, {"question": "How does this differ from an MHRA-licensed medicine containing tirzepatide?", "answer": "An MHRA-licensed medicine has been formally assessed for safety, quality and efficacy for a specific human therapeutic use and carries a marketing authorisation. This research-use product has not completed that assessment process under this listing, carries no marketing authorisation as sold here, and is supplied strictly for laboratory and in-vitro research — it is not an alternative to any licensed medicine."}, {"question": "Can this product be used in human or veterinary applications?", "answer": "No. It is sold strictly for laboratory and in-vitro research use. It is not a medicine, is not for human or veterinary use or consumption, is not licensed under the Human Medicines Regulations 2012, and has not been assessed by the MHRA for safety or efficacy under this listing."}, {"question": "Does Synedica UK ship the Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit within the UK?", "answer": "Yes, this product is offered for UK delivery to research buyers."}, {"question": "Where can I buy the Synedica Tirzepatide 40mg Injection Pen Kit for research use?", "answer": "It is available for sale directly from Synedica UK, supplied with batch documentation and third-party testing for verified UK research buyers."}]'::jsonb,
